<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cambodian People's Party</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kampuchean People's Revolutionary Party, CPP</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kampuchean People's Revolutionary Party was the ruling communist party of the People's Republic of Kampuchea, set up after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> overthrew the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-rouge">Khmer Rouge</a> in 1979 and claiming descent from the party founded in 1951. Led by former Khmer Rouge cadres such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heng-samrin">Heng Samrin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hun-sen">Hun Sen</a>, it ruled as the sole legal party under Vietnamese protection. In 1991 it abandoned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marxism-leninism">Marxism–Leninism</a> and renamed itself the Cambodian People's Party, which has dominated Cambodian politics since.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cambodian-peoples-party.jpg" alt="Cambodian People's Party" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Zachary Harden · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Cambodian_People%27s_Party.gif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Nationalists in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laos">Laos</a> believed they had to work together to free themselves from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, and formed the supranational <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indochinese-communist-party">Indochinese Communist Party</a> in 1930. The Japanese triumph in the early part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> crippled French rule and fed nationalism, and in Cambodia national pride joined with historical mistrust of the neighbouring countries. On 28 June 1951 Cambodian nationalists split from the Indochinese party to form the Kampuchean People's Revolutionary Party. In 1955 it set up a subsidiary, the Pracheachon, to contest that year's national election. The name became Workers' Party of Kampuchea on 28 September 1960 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-rouge">Communist Party of Kampuchea</a> in 1966, whose members Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norodom-sihanouk">Norodom Sihanouk</a> termed the Khmer Rouge.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>On 5 January 1979 the Cambodian communists who overthrew the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-rouge">Khmer Rouge</a> regime, ending the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a>, held a congress and re-established the Kampuchean People's Revolutionary Party. They declared themselves the true successors of the party of 1951, labelled the gathering its Third Party Congress, and did not recognise the 1963, 1975 and 1978 congresses of the Communist Party of Kampuchea. The party allied itself with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, in contrast to the pro-Chinese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pol-pot">Pol Pot</a>, and ruled the People's Republic of Kampuchea from 1979 to 1989, then the State of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a> until 1991. It took on a more reformist outlook in the mid-1980s under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heng-samrin">Heng Samrin</a>. Hun Sen was prime minister from 1985 to 1993.</p><p>In 1991, during a United Nations-sponsored peace process, it took its present name, Cambodian People's Party, with Chea Sim as president and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hun-sen">Hun Sen</a> as vice-president, abandoning the one-party system and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marxism-leninism">Marxism–Leninism</a>. It took a large part in the negotiations behind the Paris Peace Accords of 23 October 1991. In 1997 it ousted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norodom-ranariddh">Norodom Ranariddh</a> in a coup in which thirty-two people died.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The party won 64 of 123 National Assembly seats in 1998, 73 in 2003 and 90 in 2008, with 58 percent of the vote that year. It lost 22 seats in 2013. In 2018, after the main opposition, the Cambodia National Rescue Party, was banned, it won all 125 seats and 58 of 62 Senate seats. After the 2023 elections it held 120 of 125 seats. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hun-sen">Hun Sen</a> became its president in 2015, though he had held de facto control earlier.</p><p>Its leaders describe a centrist middle path between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitalism">capitalism</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communism">communism</a>, while the academic John Ciorciari observed that it kept communist-era party structures and that many top members came from the old party. Political scientists have classed it as left-wing. It belongs to the Centrist Democrat International and hosted a Socialist International meeting in 2004.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cambodian People's Party” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The PRK was a one-party state under the <b>Kampuchean People's Revolutionary Party</b>, with Vietnamese troops and advisers.</blockquote><a href="/state/peoples-republic-of-kampuchea">People's Republic of Kampuchea</a></li><li><blockquote>The ruling party, renamed the <b>Cambodian People's Party</b> in 1991, governed under Heng Samrin and Prime Minister Hun Sen while negotiating with the resistance factions.</blockquote><a href="/state/state-of-cambodia">State of Cambodia</a></li></ul></section>
